text:
Choose your profession, or roll on the table below. There are ten professions: artisan, bard, fighter, hunter, knight, mage, mariner, merchant, scholar and thief. All [[player characters|player character]] are adventurers, but you learned a thing or two before the game begins.
Your profession decides: six of your trained skills, which must be chosen from the list of the profession (step 7); the gear you can start with (step 10); your [[heroic ability]] (step 8, mages get magic instead); and it offers typical nicknames (step 4).
{{table: Profession}}

Choose your age (young, adult or old), or roll on the table below. Older age means lower [[attributes|attribute]] but more skills. Your age in years depends on your kin and has no mechanical significance.
Age changes two things: how many trained skills of your own choice you get in step 8 (two, four or six, on top of the six from your profession) and how your attributes are adjusted once you have rolled them in step 6. The adjustments do not stack, and no attribute can go above 18.
{{table: Age}}

Calculate these from your [[attributes|attribute]] and your kin:
Movement: the meters you can run in a round of combat. It starts from your kin and is modified by your AGL.
[[Damage bonus|Derived Ratings]]: you have two, one for STR-based weapons and one for AGL-based weapons.
Hit Points (HP): your maximum equals your CON (the [[heroic ability]] Robust can raise it).
Willpower Points (WP): your maximum equals your WIL (the heroic ability Focused can raise it).
{{table: Movement}}
{{table: Movement modifier}}
{{table: Damage Bonus}}

Optional. Your character is a capable individual, but even an adventurer has an Achilles heel that can get them into trouble. Roll on the table below, choose from it, or choose your weakness freely.
A weakness adds depth and personality to your character and can be used by the GM to create challenges for you. Roleplaying according to your weakness gives you an extra [[advancement mark]] at the end of the session.
{{table: Weakness}}

Optional. In addition to your other equipment you can have a memento: an item of great sentimental value that you always carry. Choose something suitable or roll on the table below.
A memento is always a tiny item with no practical use, but once per game session you can use it to recover an additional [[condition]] during a [[stretch rest]]. If you lose your memento, you may choose a new item you found on your adventures at the end of the session.
{{table: Memento}}

To attack someone in melee, you generally must be within 2 meters of your target. On the grid map you need to be positioned in a square adjacent to the enemy (including diagonally). When attacking in close combat, you use the skill that covers the type of weapon you are wielding.
{{table: Demon Roll in Melee}}
{{table: Melee Combat Actions}}

Battles are sometimes fought in places where things in the environment can be used in combat – as weapons, for protection, or for movement. These improvised weapons can be lots of different things, but what they all have in common is that they normally can only be used once, after which they are consumed. Using an improvised weapon is an action and can be combined with movement. Examples of improvised weapons can be found at the end of this book, and they are printed on cards in the boxed Dragonbane Core Set.
At the start of combat, the GM can randomly determine D3 improvised weapons that are available – or simply decide for themselves. The GM can mark an improvised weapon’s exact location on the map, or say that it is available anywhere in the area. Once used, an improvised weapon is consumed. Complete adventures can feature new and unique improvised weapons that are connected to a certain adventure site.
{{table: Inn}}
{{table: Cave}}
{{table: Forest}}

Roll D6 to see which of the tables to use: 1–3: Treasure table 1, 4–6: Treasure table 2.
{{table: Treasure Table 1}}
{{table: Treasure Table 2}}
